According to the filing, tariffs could affect Target in several ways.

**Exposure.** About one-half of the merchandise Target offers is sourced from outside the U.S., directly or through vendors, and China is the single largest source of imports. [1] Tariffs imposed or threatened in 2025 on countries including China, India, Vietnam and Bangladesh, and any retaliation by those countries, have resulted and could continue to result in substantial additional costs to procure a large portion of its merchandise. [2]

**Business effects.** These costs could affect the margin rate on certain products, lead Target to raise prices, and require it to start vendor relationships in other countries. [2] If competitors don't keep pace with price increases or offset tariffs by diversifying their vendors, Target's competitive position could be harmed. [2] The filing also notes that shifting to vendors Target is less familiar with, including to diversify geographically in response to tariffs, may raise the risk that product safety and sourcing standards are not met. [3]

**Legal uncertainty.** In February 2026 the Supreme Court ruled that IEEPA tariffs were not authorized by the statute, and the government then imposed additional global Section 122 tariffs. [2] The ruling doesn't set up a refund process, so how and when any amounts might be recovered is uncertain. Target says it cannot estimate the financial effects at this time, and that the outcome could materially affect its financial position, results of operations, and cash flows. [1]